  "answer": "Yes—for the section 43 assessment described in the supplied Manual passages.\n\nManual practice: each application is assessed in the current marketplace, not the marketplace of the past or a hypothetical future. That does not mean disregarding longstanding meanings: the Manual expressly recognises that some signs retain their connotations over long periods. A mere possibility of deception or confusion at an indeterminate time is insufficient. [TMM/Part29/3/3/2]\n\nAct: section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the trade mark, or a sign within it, makes its use for the relevant goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion. The supplied statutory text does not itself expressly state the ‘current marketplace’ direction; that direction is Manual practice. [TMA1995/s43; TMM/Part29/3/3/2]\n\nThe supplied passages do not establish that this timing direction applies to every other deception or confusion assessment."
